  <section>
    <h2>Why one gift sells for many times another</h2>
    <p>This is where most buyers go wrong. A Plush Pepe does <strong>not</strong> cost 88,888 GRAM. Four things set the price:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Model</strong> — the look of the gift. Each model shows the percentage of the collection it appears in.</li>
      <li><strong>Backdrop</strong> — the colour or gradient behind it. The scarcest backdrops carry a premium of their own.</li>
      <li><strong>Symbol</strong> — the icon repeating across the backdrop.</li>
      <li><strong>Mint number (#)</strong> — the order in which the gift was upgraded. Low and "clean" numbers (#1, #10, #100) sell higher.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Collectors call it a <strong>grail</strong> when two or three rare traits land on the same item. If a model appears 5% of the time and a backdrop 0.5% of the time, the pair together is 0.025% — a handful of items across the whole collection. With only two or three such listings on the market, the price runs many times above the floor.</p>
    <p>So when you read a number, be clear whether it is the <strong>floor</strong> (cheapest listing) or a <strong>record sale</strong>. For Plush Pepe those are 6,400 and 88,888 GRAM — one collection, about 14x apart. How traits are assigned and what upgrading costs is covered separately in the Uzbek guide to <a href="/blog/telegram-gift-collectible-upgrade">upgrading a gift to a collectible</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The rarest gift is not the most expensive one</h2>
    <p>The intuition that a smaller supply means a higher price does not survive contact with the data. From Fragment:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Heart Locket</strong> — 1,300 items, record sale 12,733.88 GRAM. Small supply meeting real demand.</li>
      <li><strong>Mini Oscar</strong> — 2,094 items, fewer than Plush Pepe, yet the record is <strong>800 GRAM</strong>: about 111x lower.</li>
      <li><strong>Scared Cat</strong> — 13,920 items, a fairly mass-market collection, but a record of 12,500 GRAM.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Demand decides, not scarcity. Plush Pepe is expensive because it became a status marker on the market, not because there are few of them. For contrast, the largest collections are Lol Pop (64,966 items), Snoop Dogg (52,452), Vice Cream (35,397) and Chill Flame (35,018).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>TON is now called GRAM — do not misread the prices</h2>
    <p>Older articles quote prices in "TON" and newer ones in "GRAM". These are not two currencies. On <strong>15 June 2026</strong>, after a TON community vote passed with 81.22% support, the token was renamed <strong>Toncoin → Gram</strong> and the ticker changed <strong>TON → GRAM</strong>.</p>
    <p>Balances, wallet addresses, smart contracts and NFTs were <strong>unchanged</strong>, and no migration was ever required from users. The network is still The Open Network. If anyone asks you for money or a seed phrase "to migrate TON to GRAM", it is a <strong>scam</strong> — see <a href="/en/blog/is-buying-telegram-stars-safe">how to spot a Telegram scam</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Buying from Uzbekistan</h2>
    <p>Buying a finished collectible on the secondary market requires a wallet funded with <strong>GRAM</strong>. Fragment and the other marketplaces do not accept Uzcard or Humo directly, which is a real barrier for most buyers here.</p>
    <p>The practical route has two steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Buy <strong>Stars or a regular gift</strong> in UZS through <a href="https://telegram.me/starsjoybot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@starsjoybot</a> — paid from Uzcard or Humo via Click or Payme, at 240 UZS per star, delivered usually within 10 seconds.</li>
      <li>Upgrade the gift to a <strong>collectible</strong> inside Telegram. The traits are assigned at random, so a rare combination is possible.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>To be straight about it: the outcome of an upgrade is <strong>chance</strong>. A rare trait is not guaranteed and we do not present this as a way to make money. Buy a gift because it is a gift; resale value is a possibility, not a plan.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the market numbers show</h2>
    <p>Fragment lists <strong>116 collections</strong> and <strong>1,281,613 items</strong> as of 12 August 2026. That is the primary source.</p>
    <p>On market size, third-party analysts put cumulative trading volume at roughly $292 million as of November 2025, across more than 2 million owners and around 9 million gifts. Telegram has not confirmed these figures, so treat them as estimates.</p>
    <p>The market is not stable. The same analysts reported months where trading volume in individual collections fell by more than 30%. Price also tracks the GRAM rate: if the token drops 20%, the dollar value of your gift drops with it even when the GRAM price is unchanged.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The takeaway</h2>
    <p>The most expensive Telegram gift is Plush Pepe #1821 at 88,888 GRAM (~1.41 bn UZS). The more useful conclusion is that the collection name does not set the price — <strong>rare traits and the mint number do</strong>. "Plush Pepe is expensive" is imprecise: <em>some</em> Plush Pepes are expensive, and the cheapest one costs 6,400 GRAM.</p>
    <p>A small supply guarantees nothing either. Mini Oscar has fewer items than Plush Pepe and a record 111x lower. Demand decides. And this is a high-risk market — treat a gift as a gift, not as an investment.</p>
  </section>

